{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_6f6a321bedfb4b7d92b4b6e2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t(ns yang.clojure\n2\t  \"Yang Clojure compiler - Compiles Clojure code into Yin VM Universal AST.\n3\t\n4\t  This is the Clojure-specific compiler in the Yang compiler collection.\n5\t  It transforms Clojure s-expressions into the Universal AST format that\n6\t  the Yin VM can execute. It handles:\n7\t  - Literals (numbers, strings, booleans, nil, collections)\n8\t  - Variables (symbols)\n9\t  - Lambda expressions (fn)\n10\t  - Function application (including stream operations via module system)\n11\t  - Conditionals (if)\n12\t  - Let bindings (let)\n13\t  - Top-level definitions via explicit yin/def store effects\n14\t  - Macro definitions (defmacro)\n15\t\n16\t  Macro support (docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md §10.2):\n17\t  - defmacro is a special form that lowers to ordinary (yin/def name (fn ...))\n18\t    syntax. The lambda node carries `:macro? true`, a frontend-internal fact\n19\t    the encoder (`yin.vm.encoder/program-batch`) turns into a declaration row;\n20\t    the canonical lambda row has no macro slot.\n21\t  - A macro call is an ordinary application. The frontend does no macro\n22\t    lookup: the row-native expander recognizes the call.\n23\t  - compile-program threads a compile-time macro-env naming the defmacros\n24\t    seen so far, only so the native defn lowering yields to a user defn macro.\n25\t\n26\t  Stream operations (stream/make, stream/put, stream/take, >!, <!) are compiled\n27\t  as regular function applications and resolved through the module system at runtime.\n28\t\n29\t  The compiler is written in .cljc format to run on both JVM and Node.js.\"\n30\t  (:refer-clojure :exclude [compile])\n31\t  (:require #?@(:jolt []\n32\t                :cljd []\n33\t                :clj [[clojure.tools.reader :as treader]\n34\t                      [clojure.tools.reader.reader-types :as rt]]\n35\t                :cljs [[cljs.tools.reader :as treader]\n36\t                       [cljs.tools.reader.reader-types :as rt]])\n37\t            [dao.jing :as jing]\n38\t            [yang.clojure.core-module :as core-module]\n39\t            [yang.clojure.prelude :as prelude]\n40\t            [yang.core.prelude :as core]\n41\t            [yang.core.uast :as u]\n42\t            [yang.tails :as tails]\n43\t            [yin.vm :as vm]))\n44\t\n45\t\n46\t;; Forward declaration for mutual recursion\n47\t(declare compile-form compile-lambda)\n48\t\n49\t\n50\t;; =============================================================================\n51\t;; Call-site occurrence contract C1 (Addendum A, design §2)\n52\t;; =============================================================================\n53\t;;\n54\t;; Every final `:application` occurrence of a produced tree carries the\n55\t;; reserved frontend-metadata key `:yang/call-site` with version value `1`\n56\t;; as a plain node key. Projection (`yin.vm/ast-side-tables`) drops the key\n57\t;; from canonical rows and emits it into the frontend-metadata side table\n58\t;; as `[origin root-address path :yang/call-site 1]`, where the first\n59\t;; three coordinates are the site id. The key carries no callee identity,\n60\t;; source arity, or ambient classification. It is distinct from\n61\t;; `:yang/site`, the safepoint mark, which travels as node metadata.\n62\t;; Dedicated node kinds (`:dao.stream.apply/call`, stream and\n63\t;; continuation nodes) are not `:application` nodes and are never marked.\n64\t\n65\t(def call-site-key\n66\t  \"The reserved frontend-metadata key of contract C1.\"\n67\t  :yang/call-site)\n68\t\n69\t\n70\t(def call-site-version\n71\t  \"The version value a C1 marker carries.\"\n72\t  1)\n73\t\n74\t\n75\t(def ^:private ^:dynamic *call-site-finalizing*\n76\t  \"True while an owning production entry point compiles a tree, so the\n77\t   recursive `compile-form` calls inside it defer marking to that entry\n78\t   point's single finalization walk over the finished tree.\"\n79\t  false)\n80\t\n81\t\n82\t(defn annotate-call-sites\n83\t  \"The finished map AST `node` with `call-site-key`/`call-site-version`\n84\t   on every `:application` node, at every depth the row grammar knows.\n85\t   Literal payloads are data, never syntax, and are not walked. Marking\n86\t   is idempotent and preserves node metadata and every other key, so\n87\t   canonical rows, roots, and executable outcomes are unchanged.\n88\t\n89\t   The walk itself is `yang.core.uast/annotate-call-sites`, shared with\n90\t   the other frontends' module trees; this frontend owns the contract's\n91\t   constants and its validation over canonical rows.\"\n92\t  [node]\n93\t  (u/annotate-call-sites node))\n94\t\n95\t\n96\t(defn eligible-application-sites\n97\t  \"The eligible C1 site set of canonical `tree` (`{:root :rows}`) under\n98\t   `origin`: one `[origin root-address path]` per `:application`\n99\t   occurrence, derived from the occurrence relation and the row tags.\"\n100\t  [tree origin]\n101\t  (into #{}\n102\t        (map (fn [[root path]] [origin root path]))\n103\t        (vm/application-occurrences tree)))\n104\t\n105\t\n106\t(defn call-site-metadata\n107\t  \"The C1 frontend-metadata rows of canonical `tree` (`{:root :rows}`)\n108\t   under `origin`: one `[origin root-address path :yang/call-site 1]`\n109\t   per eligible application occurrence, in canonical-byte order\n110\t   (`jing/canonical-compare`). This is\n111\t   the finalizer for trees that exist only as canonical rows — the\n112\t   expander's final tree, a published module's tree — where no map AST\n113\t   remains for `annotate-call-sites` to walk.\"\n114\t  [tree origin]\n115\t  (vec (sort jing/canonical-compare\n116\t             (map (fn [[root path]]\n117\t                    [origin root path call-site-key call-site-version])\n118\t                  (vm/application-occurrences tree)))))\n119\t\n120\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a","uuid":"050a0e6c-7e1a-4e9d-a729-9d4c067a7255","timestamp":"2026-11-26T12:21:07.588Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-floatkind/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc","content":"(ns yang.clojure\n  \"Yang Clojure compiler - Compiles Clojure code into Yin VM Universal AST.\n\n  This is the Clojure-specific compiler in the Yang compiler collection.\n  It transforms Clojure s-expressions into the Universal AST format that\n  the Yin VM can execute. It handles:\n  - Literals (numbers, strings, booleans, nil, collections)\n  - Variables (symbols)\n  - Lambda expressions (fn)\n  - Function application (including stream operations via module system)\n  - Conditionals (if)\n  - Let bindings (let)\n  - Top-level definitions via explicit yin/def store effects\n  - Macro definitions (defmacro)\n\n  Macro support (docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md §10.2):\n  - defmacro is a special form that lowers to ordinary (yin/def name (fn ...))\n    syntax. The lambda node carries `:macro? true`, a frontend-internal fact\n    the encoder (`yin.vm.encoder/program-batch`) turns into a declaration row;\n    the canonical lambda row has no macro slot.\n  - A macro call is an ordinary application. The frontend does no macro\n    lookup: the row-native expander recognizes the call.\n  - compile-program threads a compile-time macro-env naming the defmacros\n    seen so far, only so the native defn lowering yields to a user defn macro.\n\n  Stream operations (stream/make, stream/put, stream/take, >!, <!) are compiled\n  as regular function applications and resolved through the module system at runtime.\n\n  The compiler is written in .cljc format to run on both JVM and Node.js.\"\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [compile])\n  (:require #?@(:jolt []\n                :cljd []\n                :clj [[clojure.tools.reader :as treader]\n                      [clojure.tools.reader.reader-types :as rt]]\n                :cljs [[cljs.tools.reader :as treader]\n                       [cljs.tools.reader.reader-types :as rt]])\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [yang.clojure.core-module :as core-module]\n            [yang.clojure.prelude :as prelude]\n            [yang.core.prelude :as core]\n            [yang.core.uast :as u]\n            [yang.tails :as tails]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]))\n\n\n;; Forward declaration for mutual recursion\n(declare compile-form compile-lambda)\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Call-site occurrence contract C1 (Addendum A, design §2)\n;; =============================================================================\n;;\n;; Every final `:application` occurrence of a produced tree carries the\n;; reserved frontend-metadata key `:yang/call-site` with version value `1`\n;; as a plain node key. Projection (`yin.vm/ast-side-tables`) drops the key\n;; from canonical rows and emits it into the frontend-metadata side table\n;; as `[origin root-address path :yang/call-site 1]`, where the first\n;; three coordinates are the site id. The key carries no callee identity,\n;; source arity, or ambient classification. It is distinct from\n;; `:yang/site`, the safepoint mark, which travels as node metadata.\n;; Dedicated node kinds (`:dao.stream.apply/call`, stream and\n;; continuation nodes) are not `:application` nodes and are never marked.\n\n(def call-site-key\n  \"The reserved frontend-metadata key of contract C1.\"\n  :yang/call-site)\n\n\n(def call-site-version\n  \"The version value a C1 marker carries.\"\n  1)\n\n\n(def ^:private ^:dynamic *call-site-finalizing*\n  \"True while an owning production entry point compiles a tree, so the\n   recursive `compile-form` calls inside it defer marking to that entry\n   point's single finalization walk over the finished tree.\"\n  false)\n\n\n(defn annotate-call-sites\n  \"The finished map AST `node` with `call-site-key`/`call-site-version`\n   on every `:application` node, at every depth the row grammar knows.\n   Literal payloads are data, never syntax, and are not walked. Marking\n   is idempotent and preserves node metadata and every other key, so\n   canonical rows, roots, and executable outcomes are unchanged.\n\n   The walk itself is `yang.core.uast/annotate-call-sites`, shared with\n   the other frontends' module trees; this frontend owns the contract's\n   constants and its validation over canonical rows.\"\n  [node]\n  (u/annotate-call-sites node))\n\n\n(defn eligible-application-sites\n  \"The eligible C1 site set of canonical `tree` (`{:root :rows}`) under\n   `origin`: one `[origin root-address path]` per `:application`\n   occurrence, derived from the occurrence relation and the row tags.\"\n  [tree origin]\n  (into #{}\n        (map (fn [[root path]] [origin root path]))\n        (vm/application-occurrences tree)))\n\n\n(defn call-site-metadata\n  \"The C1 frontend-metadata rows of canonical `tree` (`{:root :rows}`)\n   under `origin`: one `[origin root-address path :yang/call-site 1]`\n   per eligible application occurrence, in canonical-byte order\n   (`jing/canonical-compare`). This is\n   the finalizer for trees that exist only as canonical rows — the\n   expander's final tree, a published module's tree — where no map AST\n   remains for `annotate-call-sites` to walk.\"\n  [tree origin]\n  (vec (sort jing/canonical-compare\n             (map (fn [[root path]]\n                    [origin root path call-site-key call-site-version])\n                  (vm/application-occurrences tree)))))\n\n","numLines":120,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2253}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"call_6f6a321bedfb4b7d92b4b6e2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
