{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026112620212577214ec0232649df","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the REPL flow: the shell reads input text via `yin.repl/read-forms` (cljs.reader on JS → bare Numbers, kind lost) and then since the clojure binding is `:input :forms :parse identity`, the already-read forms go to `clojure-lower` → `yang.clojure/compile`.\n\nThe fix must route ALL source compilation through a token-aware reader. The plan:\n\n1. A shared numeric-token classifier + carrier constructor under yang.core shared layer. Let me look at what `yang.core` is — there's `yang.core.uast`, `yang.core.prelude`. Let me check the directory.\n\n2. A focused source/token module under `src/cljc/yang/clojure/` — a token-aware reader that scans the source, tracks lexical context (comments, strings, chars, quote, metadata, dispatch, discarded forms, reader conditionals) and replaces float tokens with Float64 carriers... wait, no. Per the ruling: \"The frontend may retain token spans/kinds in a transient parse structure and materialize carrier Data from them.\" So the mechanism could be: read the source with tools.reader (as today) to get forms, and use a parallel token scan that records the positions/kinds of float tokens, then map over the read forms replacing Numbers at those occurrences with carriers. But \"No ... postwalk of read Numbers\" — the ruling says a \"postwalk of already-read Numbers\" is insufficient. Because you can't know which Number came from which token. Hmm, but if we track spans... Actually tools.reader attaches `:line`/`:column` metadata to forms read. On JVM, doubles are distinct anyway (Double vs Long) — kind is intact. The problem is only on JS. So on JS we need the token scan.\n\nWait — actually let me think about the minimal mechanism. The ruling says:\n\n\"Choose (b), frontend-owned token-aware source ingestion... 'Reconstruction' means interpreting the retained original token, not guessing from the output of an ordinary reader. It MUST occur before host maps/sets or downstream lowering can destroy occurrences. A value-keyed table, postwalk of already-read Numbers, numeric printing round-trip, or textual regex substitution over arbitrary source is insufficient.\"\n\nSo the selected architecture: a scoped source-reading adapter that consumes the original lexical information. The instruction from the orchestrator says: \"The Clojure ingestion stage (the hard piece — the ruling REQUIRES a lexical-coverage demonstration BEFORE selecting the minimal mechanism): demonstrate coverage of comments/strings/chars/quote/metadata/dispatch/discarded forms/reader conditionals + numeric tokens with the retained-kind mechanism, THEN implement.\"\n\nSo the approach: write a Clojure tokenizer (a real one, in the yang.clojure frontend) that scans source text lexically with full Clojure reader grammar coverage: whitespace/comments (; and #_..., also #! shebang?), strings, chars (\\a, \\newline, \\uXXXX...), symbols, keywords, numbers, dispatch (#{}, #{? no — dispatch macros: #{ set }, #\"regex\", #(), #'var, #_discard, #? reader conditional, #?@, ##Inf/##-Inf/##NaN), quote/syntax-quote/unquote, deref, metadata ^, and discarded forms #_ (which must be skipped including nested), reader conditionals #?(...) which read per-platform...\n\nThen two options:\n(a) A full form reader built on the tokenizer (replace tools.reader entirely) — too big.\n(b) Use the tokenizer to produce a stream of (span, kind) numeric tokens, and combine with tools.reader's position metadata to rebuild the read forms with kind restored. But forms read by tools.reader carry :line/:column metadata for lists/vectors/maps/symbols (when *read-eval* ... by default read attaches position metadata to IObj forms). Numbers are not IObj so they don't carry metadata. Hmm.\n\nActually, wait. On JVM the kind is intact (Double vs Long). On JS both are Number. So the reconstruction is needed only on JS? But the ruling says \"The revision is a portable frontend identity... Publish the same revised frontend contract on supported hosts.\" And cross-host identical artifacts. If JVM uses tools.reader (Double retained) and Node uses a token-aware reader producing carriers, results must agree — JVM `1.0` Double → float64 carrier too. The ruling: \"the Clojure source frontend MUST preserve the original numeric token's kind while source text is available and produce Jing float64 Data for every supported binary floating literal\". On JVM, Double 1.0 already means float; the frontend needs to convert Double → float64 carrier at compilation. Wait, does it? On JVM, compile-literal puts `{:type :literal :value 1.0}` where 1.0 is Double — and the encoder... let me check how :literal values are encoded. dao.jing.cbor/number-bytes at line 748 emits integer content or refuses unsafe integral Number before its float branch — \"JVM Double is float content, even at 1.0\". So JVM literals already encode as FLOAT64 in canonical bytes. The defect is only on JS where the reader collapsed to a bare Number that then encodes as INTEGER.\n\nSo actually the *frontend* fix could be: at the point where source text is available, ensure floats become Float64 carriers *on JS*. On JVM, Double already retains kind. But then cross-host byte-identity requires that the carrier (Jing Float64 on JS) and JVM Double encode to the same bytes — that's already the case since the codec handles both (Double → float64 content; JS Float64 carrier → float64 content).\n\nHmm wait, but there's a subtlety: on JVM, tools.reader returns Double for 1.0 — good. On JS, our new token-aware stage must reconstruct: read source, and for float tokens, produce `(data/float64 v)` carrier values in the form stream. Then `literal?`/`constant-form?` must admit the carrier (it's a deftype, not `number?`), compile-literal keeps it, quote keeps it, and the encoder encodes it as FLOAT64.\n\nAlternatively the frontend could