e shell's `read-forms` is still used for command detection etc., but actual compilation goes through the string.\n\nBut careful: RULING 5 says \"Do not gratuitously change an adapter that already forwards source intact\" (yin.repl.adapter). And \"equivalent REPL/preview paths\".\n\nNow, what about `lower-unit`/linked runtime? Let me look at more of yang.clojure.cljc near read-forms usage (line 2135 read-forms is the frontend's parser). Let me grep for read-forms usage across the repo.\n\nAlso the demo readers: src/cljs/datomworld/demo/{compilation_pipeline,continuation_stream,equation_plotter}.cljs.\n\nNow the big design question: the token-aware reader. Options:\n\n**Option A: full independent reader.** Write a complete Clojure reader in cljc that returns forms with floats as carriers. This replaces tools.reader on the source path. Risk: grammar coverage (all of Clojure syntax). But actually a Clojure reader is quite tractable: ~500-700 lines. The repo already values \"the reader is the parser\". But the ruling says \"It need not adopt ANTLR or replace the whole Clojure grammar. A scoped source-reading adapter can be the implementation\". So a full reader is allowed but not required.\n\n**Option B: span-indexed reconstruction.** Read with tools.reader (upstream), and independently tokenize the source to find float-token spans (start offsets). Then walk the read forms *in reading order* (which is source order for sequential structures; maps/sets need care) zipping with the token stream: every time we encounter a Number in the form tree, the next numeric token tells us its kind; if the token was float-typed, replace with carrier. This is a \"transient parse structure retaining token spans/kinds\" + \"materialize carrier Data from them\" — exactly what the ruling suggests (\"The frontend may retain token spans/kinds in a transient parse structure and materialize carrier Data from them\"). But the walking of host maps/sets: \"It MUST occur before host maps/sets or downstream lowering can destroy occurrences\" — on JS, cljs.reader reading a map literal `{1.0 :a}` produces a host map with key 1 (Number) — occurrence destroyed? If two keys `1` and `1.0` appear in the same map, the host map coalesces them (1 = 1 on JS, same hash). So reconstruction after host map construction can fail for duplicate-key coalescing. The ruling's gate 2: \"Prove that host map/set construction cannot silently discard a source occurrence because kind was erased.\" So the reconstruction must happen *before* host map/set construction — i.e., we can't use tools.reader's map reading on JS if we want to restore kinds inside maps/sets with equal-valued int/float keys.\n\nHmm. But how often is that a problem? `{1 :a 1.0 :b}` — Clojure itself on JVM throws \"Duplicate key\". On JS with cljs.reader, {1 :a, 1.0 :b} → both keys are 1, duplicate → cljs.reader actually checks duplicates and throws. What about `#{1 1.0}`? Duplicate element error. What about a map where keys are distinct but a key and a *value* are equal numbers? That's fine — positions differ, and reconstruction via source-order walking... but map iteration order! cljs.reader builds a PersistentArrayMap/PersistentHashMap from an alternating seq read in source order. When we then walk the map's keys/vals, iteration order is not source order (for hash maps). For array maps (small), order is insertion order — preserved. For maps > 8 entries → hash map, order scrambled. Then zipping Numbers with the token stream by order would misalign!\n\nUnless... we zip by value matching? That's the \"value-keyed table\" the ruling calls insufficient. Hmm, but actually with spans: tools.reader on JVM/JS attaches :line/:column metadata to forms that are IOobjs (collections, symbols), but numbers don't get metadata. BUT — we could enable position tracking differently: read the source one form at a time? No.\n\nAlternative: **make the tokenizer the reader's driver** — i.e., write our own reader for the *whole* form grammar (Option A). Then we control everything: we build maps/sets ourselves from read key/value pairs; before constructing the host map, we can keep pairs as an ordered vector structure... but wait, the compiler consumes host maps/vectors/sets. If we construct host maps with carrier keys (Float64 deftype), on JS {1 :a, (float64 1) :b} are distinct keys (different types, not =). Actually would they collide? The Float64 carrier's hashCode... The carrier refuses numeric coercion on JS (\"on JS the carrier deliberately refuses numeric coercion\"). So host map with both keys works. But `compile-constructed-collection` sorts map entries by content-root — fine.\n\nBut hold on — does the *compiler* handle a map literal with float keys? `clj/collection-key` in prelude applies \"the key admission policy\" — float content refused as collection keys at runtime (gate 2: \"Where float keys/elements are prohibited by a profile, require the same explicit refusal on both hosts\"). So compiling `{1.0 :a}` might be refused at some stage (reader/compile or runtime). The ruling says \"This ruling does not grant float-key support to Clojure-family collections: existing refusal policy remains.\" And gate 1 covers `0.0, 1.0, ...` as literals — top-level, in vectors, quoted data. For maps, float keys presumably get refused (existing policy). Let me check `clj/collection-key` in prelude.cljc:225.\n\nLet me now think about Option A scope: writing a Clojure reader covering:\n- whitespace, commas as whitespace\n- comments `;` to EOL\n- `#!` shebang comment? (Clojure 1.12; tools.reader may not support. Skip — refuse or treat as comment? Actually #! is a shebang in clojure core reader. cljs.tools.reader? Not sure. Keep same as tools.reader: probably not supported → dispatch error.)\n- strings with escapes \\n \\t \\\\ \\\" \\uXXXX \\oXXX (octal), newline escaping? (Clojure 1.12 multiline strings allowed). Keep: standard escapes.\n- chars: \\a \\newline \\space \\tab \\backspace \\formfeed \\return \\uXXXX \\oXXX\n- numbers: int (with +,-, radix 0x 0N? — 0x hex, 0 octal prefix? Clojure: 0123 is octal, 0x hex, 2r101 radix, trailing N bigint, trailing M decimal, ratio 1/2, float: decimal point, exponent e, suffix? float regex per Clojure: `([-+]?)(?:(0)|([1-9][0-9]*)|0[xX]([0-9A-Fa-f]+)|0([0-7]+)|([1-9][0-9]?)[rR]([0-9A-Za-z]+)|0[0-9]+)(N)?` for int; float: `([-+]?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]*)?([eE][-+]?[0-9]+)?)(M)?`. And ##Inf ##-Inf ##NaN.\n- symbols: including `/`, `foo/bar`, `.`, `..`, `%`, `%1`, `&`, etc.\n- keywords: `:foo`, `::foo` (auto-resolve — resolve to current ns; in our context ::foo resolution depends on ns. Hmm! What does tools.reader do in yang.clojure/read-forms? ::foo would throw \"Invalid token: ::foo\" unless *alias-map*... Actually tools.reader auto-resolves ::foo to :yang.clojure/foo? It uses rt/*ns*? The frontend probably never sees ::foo in its sources. Our reader must match tools.reader behavior for whatever the test corpus uses. Simplest: treat ::foo as auto-resolve using a fixed current-ns (the yin repl ns?). Need to check what the REPL's ns handling does. Keep behavior: use the same as tools.reader default (reads ::foo as :<current-ns>/foo where current ns is 'user or throws). Actually clojure.tools.reader/read with default opts: ::foo → \"Invalid token\" unless? In Clojure 1.7+, `::foo` in clojure.core/read-string resolves against *ns* which is user by default → :user/foo. tools.reader similarly resolves against (rt/get-file-name?) no... tools.reader uses *ns* too. cljs.tools.reader resolves ::foo against... hmm.\n\nGiven complexity, maybe restrict: our reader supports the same grammar subset the corpus and tests use, and refuses (throws) on constructs outside it? But the ruling requires \"Reader support and refusal behavior must be explicit for comments, strings, character literals, quote, metadata, dispatch forms, discarded forms, and any currently supported conditional syntax.\" So we must handle all those. `#?` reader conditionals — \"any currently supported conditional syntax\". Does the current path support #?@? tools.reader with :read-cond unspecified → throws \"Conditional read not allowed\". So current policy: reader conditionals refused. We must match: refuse them explicitly with a clear error (same as current). Metadata ^:foo — supported by tools.reader.\n\nOK. Writing a full reader is the robust path but it's a lot of code and risk. Option B has the map-order problem. Option C: **hybrid** — use our own tokenizer for lexical scanning (strings, comments, chars, dispatch...), then use tools.reader to read each *top-level* form but... no.\n\nOption D: **read with tools.reader, but pre-substitute**? \"textual regex substitution over arbitrary source is insufficient\" — rejected.\n\nOption E: **symbolic interposition** — read the source with our own reader. I keep coming back to Option A. Let me look at the existing repo: is there any Clojure tokenizer already? `yang.clojure` has reader stuff? Let me search for existing lexers in the repo. Also check `yin.repl/read-forms` and how `cljs.reader` behaves for maps >8 entries... Actually wait — one more consideration for Option B: cljs.tools.reader attaches :line/:column metadata to maps/vectors/lists/symbols as they're read, AND for map entries... no per-entry metadata.\n\nHmm, actually there's an Option B+ : zip by *reading order over the preserved seq* — when cljs.reader reads a map, it reads k1 v1 k2 v2... into a map. PersistentArrayMap preserves insertion order for ≤8 entries (with-meta). For larger, hash map order scrambled. But do we care about reconstructing inside >8-entry maps correctly? The corpus tests will exercise it if I write them to. The honest architecture per ruling is to not depend on host map order at all.\n\nActually — wait. What about reading maps as *ordered pair vectors* in our own reader, and having the compiler consume them? `compile-form` dispatches on (map? form)... The compiler consumes host data structures. If our reader produces a special \"ordered map\" type, compile-form won't recognize it. We'd need compile support. Too invasive.\n\nWhat does the compiler do with map literals? `constant-form?`/`compile-literal` for constant maps → {:type :literal :value <the host map>}. So a constant map's float values must already be carriers inside the host map at read time. For that, our reader constructs host maps but inserts k/v with carriers — insertion order preserved in the host map (array map ≤8; hash map beyond — but content is correct, only iteration order differs, which is already host-dependent behavior today and addressed by constructed-collections ordering for *dynamic* collections; for constant maps the encoded bytes sort canonically anyway).\n\nKey insight: we don't need to preserve map iteration order for *reconstruction* (Option A constructs carriers *during* reading, before host map construction — each k/v gets its carrier at its own read time). That satisfies \"MUST occur before host maps/sets or downstream lowering can destroy occurrences\" — the carrier is materialized from the token before the host map is built. Duplicate coalescing: {1 :a 1.0 :b} on JS with carrier key float64(1) ≠ 1 → both entries survive; on JVM read {1 :a 1.0 :b} → Long 1 vs Double 1.0 are not = (Clojure: (= 1 1.0) is false!) → both survive. Same behavior both hosts.\n\nSo Option A: write `src/cljc/yang/clojure/source.cljc` — a token-aware Clojure source reader. It must be *shared* between the frontend's read-forms (yang.clojure/read-forms) and the REPL (yin.repl routes :input :source through it — actually yang.clojure/read-forms IS the parse fn; the REPL binding for clojure changes from {:input :forms :parse identity} to {:parse yang.clojure/read-forms} (no :input key → source mode)).\n\nWait, but there's a catch: the REPL *shell* uses its own read-forms for command detection (yin.repl.adapter sniffs commands) — fine, unchanged. But the REPL eval path: `(compile-input state trimmed forms)` when forms-input?. If we remove :input :forms from the clojure binding, forms-input? is false → compile-source path → parse gets the trimmed string → yang.clojure/read-forms (ours). The `forms` the shell read are unused for compilation. Good. But other frontends (php/python) have :input absent already? php: {:parse php.antlr.cst/parse-source :lower ...} — no :input → source mode. Python same. So Clojure was the odd one out with :forms. Changing it to source mode matches the others.\n\nBut: `compile-input state nil [arg]` path at 2469 for compile-command-ast when forms-input? — becomes compile-source. OK.\n\nWhat breaks? Tests that rely on `:input :forms` for clojure. Let me grep tests for that. Also the multi-form eval: source string \"1.0 2.0\" → read-forms returns both; clojure-lower compiles program of 2 forms. The shell previously read N forms and passed them; now we pass the whole string — must preserve multi-form behavior (yang.clojure/read-forms already loops all forms; clojure-lower handles count 1 vs many). Good.\n\nNow — REPL *history/continuation*: line 2964 `(read-forms combined)` — for incomplete input (unclosed paren): shell accumulates lines. Let me look at that code later.\n\nNow the *reader's numeric policy*: what does the current path admit?\n- JVM tools.reader: returns Long/Double/BigInt/BigDecimal/Ratio for 1/1.0/1N/1M/1/2.\n- The compiler: literal? = number? — BigInt on JS? cljs.reader returns... for 1N cljs returns js BigInt? tools.reader 1.5.0 cljs: match-number returns (js/Number ...) etc. What happens with 1N currently on each host through yang.clojure/compile? The ruling says \"decimal M, ratio, or other unsupported categories must not be silently relabeled as binary floats... Retain the existing supported grammar/refusal policy for those categories.\" So current behavior for M/ratio/N — what is it? On JVM: (compile 1M) → literal BigDecimal → encoder number-bytes: BigDecimal isn't Double/Number? It is a Number... dao.host.integer/exact-integer? Let me check what happens. Actually gate 1 says these are \"controls\" — I need to test current behavior and preserve it. Let me probe: does yang.clojure compile `(compile 1/2)`? Probably reaches encoder and refuses or encodes weirdly. I'll probe on JVM.\n\nOur reader must reproduce the same admission: for tokens like 1N, 1M, 1/2 → same results as tools.reader on JVM? But JS differs from JVM today (cljs.reader gives Number for 1/2? No — cljs match-number: ratio → returns the ratio as... cljs has no ratio; tools.reader cljs returns a number via division? Let me check cljs.tools.reader impl/commons match-number: for int-with-N → js/BigInt? For ratio \"1/2\" → (mk-number ...) hmm.\n\nLet me just probe current behavior on both hosts as part of RED evidence, then define: our reader produces, for each numeric token class:\n- integer syntax (no float markers, no N/M suffix): exact integer → host integer (JS Number if safe; else? currently unsafe int literals like 9007199254740993 on JS → cljs reader gives Number (rounded). Ruling gate 1: \"exact-carrier large integers\" — controls. Hmm, \"Integer 0/1, exact-carrier large integers, and integer-shaped tokens are controls.\" So large integers via exact carriers (BigInt?) must stay integers. What does the current system do with 9007199254740993 on Node? Probably encodes as Number → rounded → wrong but \"existing\"? dao.jing.cbor refuses unsafe integral Numbers (\"number-bytes at line 748 ... refuses an unsafe integral Number before its float branch\"). So 9007199254740993 source on Node currently REFUSES at encode. On JVM Long works. Our reader should classify integer-shaped tokens as integers; on JS unsafe ones → we can't represent exactly as Number... The existing refusal remains (not our gate to fix — \"Retain the existing supported grammar/refusal policy\"). So: integer token → parse to host integer the same way cljs.reader does today (parseFloat for ints? cljs match-number for \"9007199254740993\" → js/parseInt? → rounded Number → encoder refuses). Keep same: our classifier marks token :integer, value = host number parse; behavior downstream unchanged.\n\nHmm wait — but BigInt support: does the repo have exact integer carriers? jing has... let me check dao.jing for bigint. There's mention \"use supported exact carriers for large integers, never a rounded JS Number\" (RULING 4). What are exact carriers? Maybe ClojureDart/bigint? Let me grep for bigint in dao.jing.\n\n- float syntax (has . or e/E, no M suffix): binary float → float64 carrier `(data/float64 v)` — THE FIX. On JVM too? If we convert Double→carrier on JVM at read time, then JVM source path also produces carriers. JVM currently produces Double; encoder treats Double as float content — same bytes as carrier. Byte-identity: preserved. And identical forms across hosts. I'll convert on both hosts (uniform). But careful: the REPL displays values; eval of literal carrier prints as? There's a render path. The ruling gate 4 wants inc/dec float behavior — carriers work there (core_impl dispatches on data/float64?).\n\nActually wait — should I convert on JVM? Consider `(compile '(+ 1.0 2.0))` — quote path: constant-form? admits... `'1.0` quoted → literal Double 1.0 on JVM today. If forms-path (non-source) still receives Doubles (e.g. yin.repl forms path when user submits via API with :lang :yang/clojure and forms input), compile must handle both Double and carrier as float content. `literal?` must admit carrier; compile-literal value = carrier; encoder: carrier → float64 bytes (same as Double). Fine — both work.\n\nDecision: our source reader emits carriers for float tokens on ALL hosts (clj/cljs; cljd/jolt throw as today — actually the reader module is only used on :default hosts; jolt/cljd keep throwing \"requires the JVM/Node reader\"... wait, but Dart? read-forms on :cljd throws. Jolt throws. OK — those hosts compile from forms; the forms path then needs carrier admission so Dart/Jolt can execute carrier-bearing artifacts (they decode from bytes anyway).\n\nHmm, actually hold on: should our reader emit carriers, or should it emit Doubles on JVM? Byte-wise identical. Forms-wise: compile-literal {:type :literal :value <carrier|double>} — the encoder encodes :value via jing. Both fine. But UAST *rows* (the tuple projection) encode the value into canonical bytes; if JVM uses Double and JS uses carrier, canonical bytes are identical (both float64). So actually we could keep JVM on tools.reader entirely (no change) and only swap the JS path! But then \"identical source strings\" produce different form objects (Double vs carrier) though identical bytes. The gate: \"Compile identical original strings on JVM and Node ... comparing kind, value, refusal behavior and canonical identity\" — compares canonical identity (bytes), not form objects. Keeping JVM on tools.reader is less code... but then the frontend has two reader implementations, and the ruling wants \"The revision is a portable frontend identity, not a Node-only variant. Publish the same revised frontend contract on supported hosts. ... JVM/Node source reading and Dart/Jolt forms execution must not be mislabeled as four identical parser capabilities.\" — it explicitly ANTICIPATES JVM/Node source reading (two hosts with source capability) and honest capability claims. So one reader used on both JVM and Node is cleanest (\"source-reader implementation identity belongs in the existing compilation derivation\").\n\nI'll write ONE portable reader (cljc, works on :clj and :cljs) used by yang.clojure/read-forms on both. On :cljd/:jolt, read-forms keeps throwing (same as today). Then JVM and Node genuinely share the same parser implementation → strongest portability claim, one implementation identity in the derivation.\n\nNow, M/N/ratio tokens: what do they do today? Probe on JVM + Node:\n- JVM tools.reader: 1N → BigInt; 1M → BigDecimal; 1/2 → Ratio (clojure.lang.Ratio).\n- compile → literal? number? true for all three. Encoder number-bytes: for BigInt — dao.host.integer/exact-integer? Let me read that file. For BigDecimal — is it Double? no. Number? yes. exact-integer? probably false → float branch → treated as double? That would RELABEL 1M as float — \"decimal M ... must not be silently relabeled as binary floats\". Hmm, but that's about *our fix* not relabeling; if the current system already relabels 1M... gate 1: \"Retain the existing supported grammar/refusal policy for those categories.\"\n\nI need to probe. If JVM encodes 1M as float64 (silently) and Node cljs.reader reads 1M as 1 (Number — actually cljs match-number for \"1M\"? cljs.tools.reader: matches int regex with M → returns (js/Number \"1\")? In cljs.tools.reader, \"1M\" → BigInt? No — cljs doesn't have decimal; I think it returns the number 1) — then today M is already host-divergent. Our reader must \"retain existing refusal policy\" — meaning we don't have to FIX M divergence, but we must not make it worse, and floats-only get carriers. Options: our reader reproduces per-host behavior via the same upstream libs for non-float numerics? No — one reader. Better: our reader explicitly REFUSES unsupported numeric categories (M, N, ratio?) with a clear diagnostic, IF that's the \"existing supported grammar\". But is refusing a regression on JVM (where 1N BigInt currently compiles fine)? Gate 1: \"exact-carrier large integers\" are controls — meaning large integers WITH exact carriers must stay integers. Does the system have an exact integer carrier that source syntax can produce today? On JVM: 1N → BigInt → encoder? Let me read dao/host/integer.cljc and dao/jing/cbor.cljc now.\n\nLet me also check what cljs.tools.reader does with 1N on Node currently: match-number in cljs returns... \"1N\" → int-string with N suffix → cljs.tools.reader returns (bigint s)? cljs has no bigint type; tools.reader cljs probably strips N and returns Number. So 1N on Node = Number 1 (integer), on JVM = BigInt 1. If encoder encodes BigInt as exact integer and Number 1 as integer — same bytes! So 1N is byte-identical across hosts today (both integer 1) as long as value fits. For huge 1N beyond 2^53: JVM BigInt exact; Node Number rounded — divergence, existing.\n\nOK here's my decision, subject to probing: our reader will:\n- classify each numeric token by syntax (integer / float / ratio / decimal-M / bigint-N / symbolic ##Inf ##-Inf ##NaN);\n- float tokens (incl. ##Inf etc.? those are floats: \"native negative zero and non-finite Numbers have float classification in Jing\" — but ##Inf on JVM tools.reader → Double/POSITIVE_INFINITY (kind intact). On JS our reader → carrier float64 Inf. Good, carrier them too.)\n- integer tokens → host integer value: on :clj Long/BigInt?; on :cljs Number via existing semantics... but our reader is one implementation producing host-appropriate values — \"one implementation; per-language lexeme dialect tables\" refers to the shared classifier. The classifier says :kind :integer/:float; the per-host materialization: :integer → parse to host integer (clj: Long if fits else BigInt; cljs: Number), :float → carrier.\n- ratio/decimal-M: what does the current system support? Probe first. If currently compiles on JVM to something, keep close: simplest honest approach — our reader refuses ratios and M decimals with an explicit \"unsupported numeric category\" diagnostic on BOTH hosts, IF current Node behavior is also effectively broken/refusing... but current JVM works for 1/2? (compile 1/2: literal Ratio; encoder: Ratio is a Number; exact-integer? false → float? (double 1/2)... need to look.)\n\nThe ruling gate 1 language: \"Preserve source numeric categories; decimal M, ratio, or other unsupported categories must not be silently relabeled as binary floats to make this gate pass. Retain the existing supported grammar/refusal policy for those categories.\" — \"existing supported grammar/refusal policy\": so whatever exists. If JVM currently silently relabels 1M→float in the encoder, that's existing behavior... but \"must not be silently relabeled\" is normative for our round. Refusing M/ratio explicitly on both hosts is the cleanest compliant reading IF they're not genuinely supported. Let me probe what happens end-to-end today: JVM (compile \"1/2\") → ast → encode → ? and Node.\n\nProbing plan (before writing tests): run a JVM REPL-ish probe with clojure -M:test -e? The memory says \"-M:test -e runs the suite first\" — trap! Let me check how to run ad-hoc code: `clojure -M:test -e '(expr)'` runs suite first? The memory says: \"-M:test -e runs the suite first\". Alternative: `clojure -X? ` or just `clj -e`? bb? There's bb.edn. Maybe `bb -e \"(require 'yang.clojure) (...)\"` — bb has the deps? Let me check bb.edn.\n\nAlso Node lane: how are tests run on Node? Look at bb.edn tasks. Let me read bb.edn and deps.edn.\n\nLet me now do a broad parallel exploration: bb.edn, deps.edn, dao/host/integer.cljc, dao/jing/cbor.cljc (201-260, 740-760), yin/vm/data.cljc (500-560), yang/clojure/core_impl.cljc (240-280), yang/clojure/prelude.cljc (200-250), typescript lower 420-450, python lower 340-370, php lower 300-320.\n\nThen the test infra: how tests run (bb tasks), the corpus test namespace, callsite test, repl tests, stream_eval test.\n\nThis is a huge round; let me get moving. I'll read many files in parallel batches.","signature":"ae2858c89f2c42308f9a7839"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a","uuid":"2d76c38b-e762-4966-9986-4046ca66481e","timestamp":"2026-11-26T12:24:06.289Z","thinking_duration_ms":157453,"thinking_display":"updates"}
