ommand — the arg is the form the SHELL read! Look at handle-command: `compile [state (render-compile-output state (compile-command-ast state (first args)))]` — args are the read forms of the command line. `(compile 1.0)` — the shell reads `1.0` as Number 1. Then compile-command-ast state 1 (not a string, not forms-input now) → throws \"This compile command expects a source string\". Hmm! So `(compile 1.0)` previously (forms-input) compiled the read form. After my change, non-string args to `(compile ...)` throw. Is that acceptable? The RULING says \"migrate actual compilation callers or explicitly delimit their legacy raw-forms behavior\". The `compile` command preview is a compilation caller. Its string form `(compile \"(+ 1.0 2.0)\")` works through source. Its form form previously compiled the shell-read form — that path loses kind anyway (it's exactly the defect). Options: keep `:input :forms` for the command preview? No — compile-command-ast with arg not string and not forms-input throws. I could make the non-string arg path still work by passing the form through when the frontend is forms-capable... but the honest fix: the `(compile ...)` command expects a source string; a bare pre-read form has no token provenance. Changing behavior: `(compile 1.0)` → error \"This compile command expects a source string\". Let me check what tests use `(compile ...)` command with non-string arg. Search test files for \"'compile\" usage.\n\nAlso demo readers: src/cljs/datomworld/demo/{compilation_pipeline,continuation_stream,equation_plotter}.cljs — audit; they may call read-forms or submit forms. Let me check them.\n\nLet me also check yang.clojure/lower-unit and how read-forms is used there (the linked runtime \"read-forms is the frontend's parser\"). grep read-forms usage in src.","signature":"f8f41a12ca5b43c1b0a3ac97"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"519cc