{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026112620273263522a8e872a4f29","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"`core_impl.cljc` is \"guest operations\" written as quoted forms (source) evaluated in a guest environment where `float/add`, `integer/add` etc. resolve via modules. So `+` in the guest library: where does `+` come from when a Clojure program runs `(+ 1.0 2.0)` through the linked/catalog path? `vm/primitives` binds `+` to host `+`. Under the argument-vector convention, `:native vm/primitive-profiles` — a bare `+` symbol compiles to a direct native call of the host `+` function. On JS, `(+ carrier carrier)` would throw (coercion refusal). RULING 2 execution obligation: the repaired profile must route floating ops carrier-aware. \"Do not change the global legacy primitive table\" — so I need a scoped binding.\n\nHow does `inc` get to `core_impl`'s version? `core-source-name` at yang.clojure.cljc:868 — \"conditional on the outcome/library convention\". Let me read that.","signature":"cd053cf0ad524d60957d9b81"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a","uuid":"fe6d5763-71ff-4698-a537-8642d7c70281","timestamp":"2026-11-26T12:27:37.922Z","thinking_duration_ms":4166,"thinking_display":"updates"}
