{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20261126202757fc1ae71d67ef4ed3","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The `operations` table has no `+`/`-`/`*`. So in a linked/outcome program, `(+ ...)` compiles via native-name? → raw host `+` primitive. The ruling's execution obligation: \"the repaired portable frontend/profile MUST route its supported floating operations through carrier-aware numeric operations/adapters, with results reboxed before becoming guest Data... Also execute supported floating +/-/* and comparisons through the actual repaired default/catalog path and linked path, including a non-integral-to-integral result.\"\n\nHmm. What's possible without touching vm/primitives (legacy table)? Options:\n(a) Add `+`/`-`/`*`/comparisons to `core_impl` operations as float-aware guest ops (like inc/dec: dispatch on data/float64? → float module ops; else integer module). Then under `::outcomes` env, core-source-name routes `+` to the library. This makes linked/catalog programs carrier-aware. The default plain-compile path (`yang.clojure/compile` without opts — \"plain-compile bindings\") would still bind raw host `+`... The ruling: \"The authorized round must inspect the default REPL and plain-compile bindings as well as linked/library paths.\" and \"Any raw-host profile retained unchanged must retain its explicit limitation and immutable identity.\"\n\nWait — what does the default REPL use? The REPL (eval path) compiles with... `clojure-lower` = `yang.clojure/compile` or `compile-program` (NO opts — native path!). Let me check what the REPL actually runs — `compile-input` → binding `:lower clojure-lower` → `(yang.clojure/compile form)` — no opts! So the REPL's standard Clojure route compiles with NO convention → free `+` resolves at runtime through :primitives (vm/primitives merged into the VM registry by the shell). Raw host `+` on JS with carriers → breaks.\n\nSo for the REPL route to work with carriers, either:\n- the shell's VM primitive table maps `+` to a carrier-aware op (that's \"the default REPL bindings\" — the ruling explicitly wants us to inspect and repair the default REPL bindings, not the global legacy table), or\n- the frontend compiles `+` to something carrier-aware under the REPL route too.\n\nWhat does `yin.repl` install as primitives? Let me check `make-vm` at line 814 and how `+` resolves. The REPL's VM is built with `:primitives vm/primitives` presumably plus modules. If I add a \"portable numeric\" adapter — like `yang.clojure.prelude` has `admit`... Look at how python clojure profile handles `+`: `install-python` with `:admit clj-runtime/admit` and `:primitives python.repl/primitives` (legacy). The clojure binding has no :primitives — so the shell's default.\n\nLet me look at yin.repl make-vm and what primitives the VM gets.","signature":"45e2496cd193400482e80503"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a","uuid":"b5a7a726-26a4-476b-b004-99cedc4cc14b","timestamp":"2026-11-26T12:28:10.254Z","thinking_duration_ms":11299,"thinking_display":"updates"}
