Doubles today? Host + → Double. After my change on JVM REPL: dispatch — a Double 1.0 is cbor/float64? → float/add → data/float64 result → JVM Double. Same result. Integer 1 → host + → Long... but via integer/add + impl-int-result? On JVM host `+` with small longs is identical to integer/add. Careful: `+` with 0 args → 0; 1 arg → identity; 2+ → variadic fold. The REPL override must preserve variadic arities (Clojure + is variadic). float/add is binary [2]. Fold pairwise.\n\nAlso non-numeric operands: Clojure `(+ \"a\" 1)` throws ClassCastException. Our adapter: if neither float nor integer → fall back to host op (which throws as before). Good — preserves refusal behavior.\n\nComparisons on JVM today: `(< 1 2)` → host <. Mixed `(< 1 1.5)` → host < → true. With adapters: either float → float compare of double values → same. NaN: Clojure `(< ##NaN 1)` → false. float/compare returns ::nan sentinel → lt → (= -1 ::nan) → false. Good.\n\n`=`: host `=` on JVM vs data/equiv — for numbers/vectors/maps/strings/keywords identical outcomes for supported data. (= 1/2 1/2)? Ratio — data/equiv: exact-int? no, float64? no... falls to keyword? symbol? sequential? map? set? :else (= a b) → host =. OK identical.\n\nBUT: overriding `=` in the REPL primitive table changes `=` for ALL languages' REPL sessions (php legacy has its own :primitives; python legacy too; the clojure binding doesn't install :primitives so make-repl-primitives serves all frontends without their own table — PHP/python clojure profiles use :admit clj-runtime/admit with no :primitives?... install-python: legacy? gets :primitives python.repl/primitives; non-legacy gets :admit. So the clojure profile (python clojure-core-v1) ALSO uses the shell's make-repl-primitives!). Changing `+` there affects the Python Clojure profile REPL route too. Is that bad? The Python Clojure profile compiles Clojure-source programs with clojure semantics — its `+` is Clojure's `+`. Carrier-aware dispatch only changes behavior for float64 carriers (previously: raw host op — on JS they'd throw; on JVM Double works same). So the override is behavior-preserving except where it fixes breakage. Good.\n\nHmm, wait — actually for Python-clojure profile, clj.py/add etc. handle `+`? No — that's for Python SYNTAX programs (python.repl lowers python ASTs to clj.py/add calls). The Clojure-profile python frontend compiles Clojure forms via yang.clojure — `+` free name → shell primitives. OK.\n\nNow the linked path: unit-opts `:native vm/primitive-profiles` — `+` native-name? → BUT core-source-name takes precedence (checked first in native-name?): `(not (core-source-name env sym))` — so if I add `+` to core_module/source-names (via public-names), under outcomes env `+` routes to the library callable, not the raw primitive. In the library, `+` body uses integer/float module ops — carrier-aware. \n\nNow — IMPORTANT scope check: does adding `+ - * ...` to core_impl change the clojure.core.impl artifact bytes → module pins? Yes. RULING 3: \"a change to shared clojure.core.impl/clojure.core/clj artifacts requires new pins for every consuming profile whose closure changes; never mutate an existing clojure-core-v1 publication in place.\"\n\nWhere are these pins? python.profiles/builtin — descriptors with addresses. Let me look at how the clojure library is pinned in python profiles (the \"clojure-core-v1\" pin) and what re-pinning involves. This could be the heaviest part (gate 7). Let me look at yang.python.antlr.profiles and where module addresses come from.","signature":"8eeba15675fb4a8ba6156351"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a","uuid":"8b9304a1-203c-4a50-b0f0-e396eebc173a","timestamp":"2026-11-26T12:29:32.671Z","thinking_duration_ms":25992,"thinking_display":"updates"}
