out profiles. Let me check how a linked unit executes in tests — search for lower-unit usage in tests.\n\nAlso: what about `=` compiled under outcomes → `=` is in vm/primitives (host =) — under outcomes env, core-source-name? `not=` is in the library; `=` is not (not in public-names? inc/dec/not=/... let me check: operations include not= but not =). So `=` native → host = → with carriers on JS breaks. My override in make-repl-primitives fixes the REPL/linked paths served by the shell.\n\nFor test lanes that build VMs with vm/primitives directly and eval source-read forms — my new tests will use the REPL route (eval-input through a session) or construct VMs with the repaired table. For the corpus semantic oracle, use data module ops.\n\nOK — but one more check: `vm/primitives` itself — is overriding in make-repl-primitives enough for \"the default REPL\"? make-repl-primitives is used at line 855 (make-vm) and 2391 (rebuild). Also embed hosts may construct VMs with vm/primitives directly — \"Any raw-host profile retained unchanged must retain its explicit limitation and immutable identity\" — fine, vm/primitives stays unchanged (legacy raw-host profile), documented.\n\nNow what about `yang.clojure/compile` of `(+ 1.0 2.0)` in a bare test VM with vm/primitives? That's the raw-host profile — documented limitation. Tests for execution use the repaired route.\n\nHmm — wait, actually let me reconsider whether to put the carrier-aware `+` into `vm/primitives` itself... NO — ruling explicitly: \"do not change the global legacy primitive table or Float64 coercion policy incidentally\". Keep vm/primitives untouched. \n\nDecision: Plan A. The repaired bindings:\n1. `yin.repl/make-repl-primitives` — numeric overrides (carrier-aware, both hosts) — this is THE default REPL binding.\n2. Linked/catalog execution through the shell uses the same table.\n3. `clojure.core.impl` untouched → no python profile re-pin for that. \n\nHmm, but hold on: linked modules when run \"cold\" (not thr