{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20261126203137ec99982f1af8443a","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Confirmed the defect root, exactly as the ruling derived:\n- `match-number`: int-pattern → js/parseInt (Number); float-pattern → js/parseFloat (Number — kind lost); ratio-pattern → `(/ (parseInt num) (parseInt den))` — **ratio silently becomes a Number** (integer when it divides evenly! `1/2` → 0.5, `4/2` → 2 — an INTEGER!). And `10.03M` → parseFloat → Number (float content, non-integral stays float but \"2.0M\" → 2 → integer!). Also `1N` → match-int drops the N (m 8 group ignored — no BigInt!) → js/parseInt → 1.\n\nSo current JS behavior: N suffix dropped (no bigint carrier), M → parseFloat, ratio → division.\n\nCurrent JVM behavior: 1N → BigInt, 1M → BigDecimal, 1/2 → Ratio.\n\nSo for those categories today: JVM encodes bigint→integer bytes (large ok), BigDecimal→tag 4, Ratio→tag 30. JS: 1N→integer Number; 1M→float or integer Number; 1/2→float or integer Number. Existing divergence.\n\nNow, the ruling gate 1: \"Preserve source numeric categories; decimal M, ratio, or other unsupported categories must not be silently relabeled as binary floats to make this gate pass.\"\n\nDesign decision for our reader (both hosts, ONE implementation):\n- `1/2`-shaped ratio tokens → construct a RATIONAL carrier: JVM clojure.lang.Ratio, JS dao.host.number Rational carrier. This preserves the category on both hosts and matches today's JVM bytes exactly. Cross-host: JS upgrades from broken Number to true rational — new capability, no regression. Encoder handles both (cbor/ratio? → tag 30).\n- `1M` decimal tokens → JVM BigDecimal, JS Decimal carrier (dao.host.number has decimal? — need constructor; check).\n- `1N` bigint tokens → JVM BigInt, JS js/BigInt (exact-integer? carrier). Encoder int-bytes both.\n- plain int tokens → JVM Long (via Long/parseLong, BigInt when overflow), JS Number via parseInt (retain rounding/ref