{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202611262033538858efab85c94be8","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Demo sources: plain arithmetic with `0.05` floats, `sin`, `dao.stream.apply/call`. `(sin x)` — a free symbol resolved from... math-primitives merge. Note `(+ x-min 0.05)` — raw + with a float! On JS after the reader fix, 0.05 → carrier → raw + breaks the demo → my REPL-primitives fix pattern is needed in the demo VM too? The demos build their own VM `{:primitives (merge vm/primitives math-primitives)}` — raw host +. With carrier literals, `(+ x-min 0.05)` breaks on JS. The demos are .cljs — they need the carrier-aware numeric adapters merged too. The ruling's audit list includes the demos. So: export the numeric adapter table (e.g. `yang.clojure.prelude/numeric-primitives` or a new shared fn) and merge it in the demo VMs.\n\nHmm wait — actually, is `(sin x)` — sin from math-primitives (host Math/sin) — returns Number (non-integral float typically — fine as Number float content? Number 0.123 is float64? on JS — cbor/float64? true for non-integral Numbers! OK). But (+ carrier-number-mix) breaks. So yes — merge adapters into demo VMs.\n\nNow — where should the carrier-aware numeric adapter FNs live so yin.repl, and the demos (cljs only), and tests can all use them?\n- Demos are cljs; they require yang.clojure (as yang). A var in yang.clojure.prelude? prelude.cljc is on all hosts incl. jolt/cljd. The adapters as host fns: `(fn [& xs] ...)` dispatching cbor/float64?/exact-integer? → yin.vm.float kernels (data/float64, float-value). These are plain host functions — fine on jolt/cljd too (never called there).\n- Put them in `yang.clojure.prelude` (already required by yang.clojure/frontends and the demos? demos require yang.clojure which requires prelude). Export `numeric-primitives` map {'+ ... '- ... '* ... '= ...}.\n\nHmm — but hold on: is prelude.cljc in the audit boundary? RULING 5 boundaries: \"Audit src/cljc/yang/core/{uast,defquoted}.cljc, src/cljc/yang/clojure/{prelude,core_impl,core_module}.cljc ... Changes there require a demonstrated producer gap.\" — prelude IS in the audit set and changes require a demonstrated gap. The numeric adapters are the execution-reboxing integration — RULING 2 execution obligation explicitly authorizes \"scoped frontend/runtime bindings\" and the file boundaries include repl.cljc + the audit set. The producer gap demonstrated: source-float literals become carriers → raw host + breaks (I'll show RED). That's the demonstrated gap justifying the prelude/repl change. Good — document exactly that way.\n\nAlternatively put adapters in yin.repl directly... but demos can't require yin.repl? They could (yin.repl is cljc, loadable in browser? demo requires yin.vm already). Hmm, yin.repl is heavy. prelude is the right home (it's the clj runtime module namespace — \"runtime\" bindings).\n\nWait, actually — one more consideration. `yang.clojure.prelude` requires yin.vm.float already (line 11: [yin.vm.float :as float]). It's the natural home.\n\nNow the CATALOG route (gate 1: \"frontend source entry and the standard REPL/catalog route\"): the standard catalog's clojure binding — change {:input :forms :parse identity} to {:parse yang.clojure/read-forms}. This is in frontends.cljc — in boundaries.\n\nNow think about what happens to `yin.repl/read-forms` (the shell reader): stays as-is (command sniffing + completeness). It reads with cljs.reader/clojure.core read-string. Note: the shell reads EVERY submission to detect commands; a source string with reader features our shell-reader handles differently (e.g. \"1/2\" JVM → Ratio for sniffing — fine, only for command detection). BUT — eval-parsed* checks `(command-form? form)` etc. from the SHELL's forms — and then compiles via compile-input with OUR reader. If our reader throws on something the shell reader accepted (e.g. ratio now refused), the error surfaces from compile — fine.\n\nHmm — one subtlety: `ast-map?` / `datom-stream?` checks on shell-read forms — those are special shell syntaxes (AST maps submitted directly). Unchanged.\n\nBut WAIT — there's a REAL trap: the shell reader (cljs.reader/clojure.core read-string) must still PARSE the input for command detection. If input is \"(sin 1.5)\" fine. What about input our shell reader can't read but our frontend reader can (or vice versa)? E.g. cljs.reader vs our reader on `#?` — both refuse. `::foo` — clojure.core/read-string accepts (:user/foo), our reader refuses → compile error. The shell read SUCCEEDS, command detection says not-a-command, compile-input called → our parse throws → error shown. OK — behavior: refusal at compile. Good.\n\nWhat about shell read FAILING but our reader succeeding? cljs.reader is strict-ish. e.g. \"1/2\" JVM shell → Ratio (fine). `#'x` shell → (var x) fine. `#{1}` fine. If shell read throws → {:error ...} → for forms-input frontends error path; for source-mode (us): `(forms-input? state)` false → `:else (eval-compiled state (compile-source state trimmed))` → our reader re-parses; if we succeed where cljs.reader failed — evaluates fine (nice bonus: better error tolerance? NO WAIT — risky divergence, but acceptable).\n\nHmm, actually careful — clojure.core/read-string reads ONE form; yin.repl/read-forms wraps in [...] to read all. `(read-string \"[1 2 not closed\")` → throws → parsed {:error}. Then since not forms-input, compile-source re-parses with our reader → also throws (unclosed) → error text. Consistent.\n\nNOW — the BIG remaining piece to understand: how tests drive the REPL (for gate 1's \"standard REPL/catalog route\" and gate 4's \"default/catalog path\"). Look at an existing repl test using eval-input. Let me look at test/yin/repl/state_test.cljc or embed_test for the eval pattern.","signature":"1894484e352f44ba8951dc67"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"sessi