{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20261126203605830a7e89b9124329","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good picture of the compiler. Let me now check the remaining flows quickly:\n\n1. How the encoder treats a :literal with a carrier — the TS path already does `(u/lit (cbor/float64 v))` so :value with carrier works through vm/ast->semantic-bytecode → encoder. Confirmed by precedent.\n\n2. `compile-literal` — leaves :value as-is → carriers pass through if `literal?`/`constant-form?` admit them. That's the carrier admission fix: extend `literal?`/`constant-form?` to admit `(cbor/numeric? x)`... careful: cbor/numeric? includes unsafe integral JS Numbers? host-integer? includes unsafe integral Numbers (for refusal) — number? already admits those via literal? today (they refuse at encode). Admitting cbor/numeric? adds: carrier Float64, Rational, Decimal, BigInt(js). Fine — encoder refuses unsafe integral Numbers exactly as today.\n\nBut careful about maps with carrier VALUES inside constant collections: constant-form? walks keys/vals with number? checks — a carrier value inside a vector → (number? carrier) false → not constant → falls to compile-constructed-collection → conj path — evaluates fine at runtime (carriers as values OK). But we WANT constant collections with carriers to stay :literal (constant fast path) — e.g. `[1.0 2.0]` source → vector of carriers → should be a literal vector, not a conj construction! So constant-form? must treat carriers as constants. Similarly quoted `'([1.0])`. Extend both predicates.\n\nAlso `compile-constructed-collection`'s sort uses content-root on children — works for any compiled child.\n\n3. Key positions: map literal `{1.0 :a}` source → our reader builds host map {carrier :a} → constant? → compile-literal → encoder: map with Float64 key → admit? At ENCODE time: cbor encode of map keys — does the codec refuse float keys? Jing canonical encode of a map —