sts. Gate 2's \"host map/set construction cannot silently discard a source occurrence because kind was erased\" — PROVEN by construction (carriers materialized before host map build).\n\n4. What about `read` ERRORS parity: tools.reader throws ex-info with :type :reader-exception etc. Our reader throws ex-info \"read error\". Tests asserting specific reader error messages? The shell formats \"Error: <msg>\". Fine.\n\n5. Metadata: tools.reader attaches {:line :column} meta to read forms (with an indexing reader). Current yang.clojure/read-forms uses plain string-push-back-reader (NOT indexing) → NO line/column meta today! So arity-map test's \"anonymous-with-source\" builds meta manually. Our reader: attach no position meta (match current behavior) — simpler. BUT compile-form-1 merges (meta form) into ast meta — with no meta, unchanged. Good: skip position metadata entirely (current behavior).\n\n6. gensym-free: no #() → no gensyms needed. Syntax-quote: REFUSE (decision above — verify nothing in repo source-strings uses `...` — the demos' DEFAULT sources don't; check test sources with read-forms-strings later: grep '(read-forms \"' tests).\n\n7. Naming: reserved `dsg__` check — check-program-symbols! runs over forms; reader output unaffected.\n\n8. what about `(defn f [x] ...)` docstrings & attr-maps — read normally.\n\n9. Conditionals #?(:clj ...) — refuse (\"Reader conditional not allowed\" — matching tools.reader default). Actually tools.reader default message: \"Conditional read not allowed\". Use similar wording + our diagnostic key.\n\n10. Character literals — must handle carefully: \\a, \\newline, \\space, \\tab, \\backspace, \\formfeed, \\return, \\uXXXX (exactly 4 hex), \\oNNN (3 octal ≤ 0377). Multi-char invalid → error \"Unsupported character\".\n\n11. Symbols: token chars until terminating macro/whitespace. Symbol validation per parse-symbol (no leading digit unless... numbers are matched first; token starting with digit → try number match; if no number pattern matches → \"Invalid number: <token>\" error). Tokens like `1.2.3` → invalid number error. `+foo`/`-foo` symbols (leading +/- followed by non-digit → symbol). `foo/bar` symbol with ns. `/` alone symbol. `a/b/c` invalid.\n\n12. Keywords: `:foo`, `:foo/bar`, `::foo` refuse. `:/`? weird. Keep validation: token after : must parse as symbol-ish; empty → error.\n\n13. Also `^` meta reading: `^:adv x`, `^{:a 1} x`, `^\"s\" x`? (string meta → :str? no — strings not valid meta in Clojure... actually ^\"x\" attaches? In Clojure, meta must be Symbol/Keyword/String/Map? — Clojure's reader: ^String x (symbol → :tag), ^{:a 1}, ^:kw, ^\"str\"? I think ^\"str\" attaches {:tag \"str\"}? Yes! (meta ^\"java.lang.String\" x) works — String meta becomes :tag? Hmm: Clojure reader read-meta: if String → {:tag s}? Actually (read-string (pr-str (meta (read-string \"^\\\"java.lang.String\\\" x\")))) — I recall strings allowed as :tag. Keep: symbol → {:tag sym}, keyword → {kw true}, map → itself, string → {:tag s}. tools.reader same.\n\n14. Numbers immediately followed by weird chars — token-based scan: read token [^whitespace@^`~()[]{}\"\\;,]+ then classify: number-ish (starts with digit, or +/- digit) → number patterns; else symbol.\n\n15. `,` is whitespace.\n\n16. Comments `;` to newline. Also `#!`? Clojure 1.12 added #! line comments? tools.reader 1.5.0 supports #!? Not sure — skip (refuse as unknown dispatch, matching tools.reader if it doesn't support).\n\n17. `#_` discard — recursive skip (read and drop the next form — must parse it fully to skip correctly).\n\n18. `#'` var → (var x).\n\n19. `@` → (deref x). `~`/`~@` outside syntax-quote → error. `` ` `` → REFUSE (explicit diagnostic).\n\n20. `#<` → \"Unreadable form\".\n\n21. `#{`, `#(` → `#(` REFUSE explicit. `#{` set. `#\"` regex → re-pattern per host. `#?`/`#?@` refuse. `##` symbolic values Inf/-Inf/NaN. `#'`. Unknown dispatch → error.\n\n22. Also eof handling: read-forms loop until eof.\n\n23. Namespaced maps `#:foo{...}` — Clojure 1.9+ feature; tools.reader 1.5.0 supports #:ns{} — do we? Current system reads them via tools.reader. Rarely used here. Support or refuse? REFUSE with explicit diagnostic (namespaced map literal not supported) — hmm, that regresses. But implementing is ~15 lines (prefix all bare keys with ns). Implement it? The ruling demands explicit support-or-refusal per syntax class; namespaced maps weren't in its enumeration (\"comments, strings, character literals, quote, metadata, dispatch forms, discarded forms, conditional syntax\"). I'll implement it (simple) — no wait, scope discipline + time. Check usage: grep \"#:\" in repo source strings... Let me check quickly. If unused → explicit refusal, documented.\n\nOK — and CRITICALLY: the shared numeric classifier (yang.core.numerics). The Clojure reader calls it with a token; dialect :clojure. It returns kind + parsed carrier. ts/php/python adapters switch to it for their float/int split decisions.\n\nLet me look at how ts/php/python currently decide float-ness:\n- TS: number-literal — cond 0x/0o/0b radix-double; legacy octal error; else host-double(decimal-text) → ALWAYS float64 (every TS Number literal is float). BigInt literal separate. So the \"classifier\" for TS = \"is this a Number (non-BigInt) literal\" — trivially always float. The dialect table: TS has no int-vs-float distinction in tokens (except BigInt suffix n). The shared classifier's role for TS: token kind :float for all Number tokens (dialect declares \"all Number literals are float64\"), :bigint for n-suffixed. Adapter call: replace the cond's parse dispatch? \"do not rewrite beyond the adapter call\" — keep radix handling (TS-specific: 0x floats!) in the lowerer; the classifier provides the shared predicate + carrier. Minimal TS change: `numerics/float-literal ts-dialect text` → carrier (host-double parse stays?)... hmm. The REAL shared piece: **the carrier constructor** `numerics/float-carrier` = #(cbor/float64 (host-parse %)) with host-parse per host (Double/parseDouble vs js/parseFloat vs double/parse) — all three lowerers duplicate exactly this: `#?(:cljd (double/parse t) :clj (Double/parseDouble t) :cljs (js/parseFloat t))` → `(cbor/float64 ...)`. THAT's the shared factory: one `numerics/float64-of-text [s]` + `numerics/int-of-text`, + the classifier `numerics/classify [s dialect]` where dialect says which token shapes are float vs int. TS: dialect {:float-tokens :all-number} (classification handled by its own radix logic); Python: float iff [.eE] and not 0x — `numerics/float-token? s py-dialect`; PHP: has its own grammar rules (php float tokens: `.`, `e`, also hex floats are ints in PHP!) — php float-literal called from its own dispatch; Python's parse-number calls shared float-token? + numerics/float64-of-text; TS keeps radix branches but the decimal branch calls numerics/float64-of-text; PHP float-literal → numerics/float64-of-text.\n\nThe Clojure dialect: classifier distinguishing int (with radix/suffix N), float (with M suffix → :decimal), ratio. The Clojure-specific grammar lives in the reader; the classifier's shared core: given text + dialect spec {int-suffix?, float-markers, decimal-suffix?, radix-prefixes, ratio?} → kind. To keep ONE implementation: implement `classify` generically over a dialect map:\n\n```cljc\n(def dialects\n  {:clojure {:radix-prefixes {\"0x\" 16 \"0X\" 16 ...} ...}})\n```\n\nHmm — generic-enough-but-not-overfitted. Let me define the shared API:\n\n```cljc\n(ns yang.core.numerics ...)\n(defn host-double [s] #?(:cljd (double/parse s) :clj (Double/parseDouble s) :cljs (js/parseFloat s)))\n(defn float64-literal [s] (cbor/float64 (host-double s)))   ;; the carrier constructor\n(defn float-token? [s dialect-key] ...)                     ;; lexeme dialect table\n(defn classify [s dialect-key] ...)                          ;; :integer/:float/:bigint/:decimal/:ratio/:symbolic-float/:invalid\n```\n\nDialect tables:\n```cljc\n(def ^:private lexemes\n  {:clojure  {:int-suffix \\N :decimal-suffix \\M :ratio? true :float-marks #{\\. \\e \\E} :radix #{16 8 \\r} ...}\n   :python   {:float-marks #{\\. \\e \\E} :radix-prefixes {\"0x\" \"0o\" \"0b\"} :suffixes \\{\\_ sep}}\n   :php      {...}\n   :typescript {...}})\n```\n\nPython's current check: `(and (not (re-find #\"^0[xob]\" lower)) (re-find #\"[.e]\" lower))` — float iff contains . or e (case-insens via lower) and not radix-prefixed. PHP: its float-literal is called from the grammar's own dispatch (ANTLR token types Decimal/Hex/Binary/Octal for ints; float tokens separately typed by ANTLR!) — PHP's grammar tokenizes float vs int itself; lower.cljc float-literal receives already-classified float text. So for PHP the classifier isn't even consulted — only the carrier constructor is shared. TS: same (Number vs BigInt token types) — constructor shared.\n\nSo the SHARED piece across all four: (1) carrier constructor from decimal text (host parse + cbor/float64); (2) a token classifier with per-language dialect tables — genuinely used by Clojure (full classification) and Python (float-token? for the .e check); TS/PHP keep ANTLR-driven classification and share the constructor. That satisfies \"one implementation; per-language lexeme dialect tables\" without rewriting the controls beyond the adapter call.\n\nNow let me also confirm the yang.core layer's dependency direction: yang.core.prelude requires what? Check quickly that dao.jing.cbor is acceptable there.","signature":"43fc7c1ea4464b8b88400bf8"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a","uuid":"0b6f030d-d7b8-4c87-a610-35f8be6d4031","timestamp":"2026-11-26T12:37:19.305Z","thinking_duration_ms":71718,"thinking_display":"updates"}
