{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202611262038173571f83524684949","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The `1/2` in my keyword `:read-1/2` — the -e string itself is read by Clojure... `:read-1/2` is fine as keyword. Error \"Invalid token: :read-1/2\"?? Weird — oh! The outer shell double quotes: my Clojure code contains `(yang/read-forms \\\"1/2\\\")` — that's fine. \"Invalid token: :read-1/2\" — hmm, this is the REPL reading MY -e code... `:read-1/2` IS a valid keyword (ns \"read-1\", name \"2\")... unless the argument got mangled by shell. zsh + parentheses — I quoted with double quotes so $(...) etc. wouldn't expand... `(format \\\"%02x\\\" %)` contains `%` fine. Wait \"Invalid token: :read-1/2\" — maybe the error is from `(println :read-1/2 ...)` where... no, keywords with / are valid. Unless... the outer code passed through `clojure -M -e` — hmm, let me simplify: avoid slashes in keywords entirely.","signature":"90e86efb666b43719788c1d8"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a","uuid":"424dc198-77d7-42c1-896f-468e508b8e71","timestamp":"2026-11-26T12:38:24.405Z","thinking_duration_ms":4870,"thinking_display":"updates"}
