ted (shell-side).\n4. `recheck-pending` / fold-queued — passes :trimmed strings. Fine.\n5. dht/remote paths? grep for compile-input/compile-source callers... only these.\n6. `eval-compiled` staging — same.\n\nAlso php/python bindings unchanged.\n\nWhat about REPL sessions evaluating MULTIPLE forms per line \"1.0 2.0\"? compile-input → :parse reads ALL forms → clojure-lower count>1 → compile-program. Previously the shell read all forms and passed the vector. Same.\n\nWhat about `(eval-input state \"\")` empty → read-forms [] → parsed {:forms []} → forms falsy → forms-input? false → :else compile-source \"\" → our reader → [] → clojure-lower [] → compile-program []? count 0 → compile-program with empty forms... previously: forms=[] → forms branch `(forms ...)` — empty seq is truthy in Clojure! `(if forms ...)` — [] is truthy → compile-input state trimmed [] → :parse [] (identity) → clojure-lower [] → compile-program []. SAME. Good.\n\nNOW — the shell reader's own limits: yin.repl/read-forms uses cljs.reader (JS) / clojure.core/read-string (JVM) / edn (cljd). Clojure.core/read-string has *read-eval* concerns: `(read-string \"#=(unsafe)\")` — eval during read! That's the CURRENT shell behavior (JVM). Not my scope (shell sniffing only) — unchanged.\n\nNEXT — check test/yang/clojure_test.clj (JVM-only) — it tests read-forms/compile behavior that might break. And stream_eval_test + callsite_test for source-string usage. Let me grep for read-forms usage in tests.","signature":"fefa814315aa4184ba5508cf"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a","uuid":"f15702bc-5b3f-411a-92a5-787414ccd063","timestamp":"2026-11-26T12:40:27.459Z","thinking_duration_ms":24983,"thinking_display":"updates"}
