{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20261126204714f60440ccd4c34814","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see the pattern: `(shell/eval-input (repl.frontends/create-state {:vm-type vm-type}) line)` → [state text]; output-elements drains the output stream for the :repl/result value.\n\nFor my test namespace, the REPL route helper:\n\n```cljc\n(defn- repl-eval\n  [line]\n  (let [[state _] (repl/eval-input (frontends/create-state {:vm-type :semantic}) line)]\n    [state (last (for [e (output-elements state) :when (= :repl/result (:type e))] (:value e))])))\n```\n\nI need output-elements (drain) — replicate the ~10 lines (dao.stream :as stream required).\n\nWhich vm-type? :semantic is the default. main_test vm-types — what are they? [:ast-walker :semantic :stack :register]? Probably. Use :semantic.\n\nNow, big content plan for the test file. Given the timebox, I'll write it in sections via multiple python heredoc appends. Sections:\n\nS0: ns + helpers (compile-source-snapshot, digest, repl route, artifact helpers).\nS1: gate 1 — numeric token kinds through frontend source entry (+ recorded JVM reference digests — placeholder zeros now? NO: for RED, I write assertions that don't need recorded refs: e.g. (is (cbor/float64? v)), (is (= <float64-tag-bytes> (jing/canonical-bytes v))) — host-independent self-truths. Cross-host identity: compare canonical-bytes digest to a JVM-recorded constant — I record during GREEN and fill in. For RED, use a recorded table filled with POST-fix values — but that makes RED trivially fail (fine — it IS red). Cleaner: gate-1 assertions are semantic (float64? + exact value + data/equiv), and a separate cross-host digest table test filled post-fix. I'll write the semantic assertions now (RED on Node: float64? false → fail; RED on JVM: PASS pre-fix mostly — that's OK, red evidence is per-host).\n   Hmm — wait. On JVM PRE-fix, read-forms returns Double → compile literal Double → (cbor/float64? 1.0-Double) → true on JVM. So gate 1 JVM passes pre-fix. The JVM RED comes from: carrier admission tests (explicit carriers through compile — FAILS pre-fix \"Cannot compile unknown form type\"), REPL text rendering, gate 7 revision, corpus source rows (Double vs recorded... no). Let me ADD gate-1 JVM-red assertions: the forms returned by read-forms must be THE CARRIER DATA (data/float64? of the form value on JS; on JVM Double — same). For \"identical forms across hosts\" I can assert (jing/canonical-bytes form) equality against recorded JVM digests — recorded post-fix. During RED these fail on both hosts (constant :pending). I'll put the digest table in with placeholder \"unrecorded\" markers guarded so RED shows them failing... simpler: write the digest-comparison deftest LAST during green when recording. For RED, include the token semantic table + structural tests now.\n\nS2: gate 2 — structural + lexical coverage + occurrence preservation + refusal parity.\nS3: gate 4 — runtime semantics oracle (REPL route + data module).\nS4: gate 5 — carrier forms admission, execution, serialization; prelude audit; snapshot crossing.\nS5: gate 6 — cross-host artifact decode/re-encode/execute (embedded post-fix fixture — record during green), legacy custody (pre-fix fixtures — EXISTS NOW), capability claims (cljd/jolt refusal — via a var? on clj hosts can't test jolt's throw... skip to jolt leg).\nS6: gate 7 — manifest revision/profile assertions, unchanged versions, immutable python pins.\n\nCorpus extension (gate 3) goes into clojure_collection_order_test.cljc separately.\n\nWriting ALL of this is a lot. Let me be surgical and write compact but real tests. Start writing. I'll draft the whole file in one go, carefully.\n\nImportant details:\n- `yang/read-forms` currently exists (tools.reader). RED tests call it — on Node pre-fix it returns Numbers → float64? false → RED.\n- REPL route: pre-fix on Node — \"1.0\" → 1 → result 1 → data/float64? false → RED. On JVM → 1.0