{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202611262055352c4663a0d9a4417a","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The stream_eval harness is the perfect model for \"a float result crossing a round boundary\" — `a-session-threads-definitions-streams-and-history` (line 337) threads definitions across rounds. For gate 5's snapshot/continuation: a parked stream round IS a continuation crossing! Look at \"a-read-on-an-empty-stream-parks-and-the-round-is-consumed\" — the round parks (VM state captured in :pending-run) and later resumes. A float-producing operation whose result is integral crossing that boundary: program reads from a stream (parks), computes (+ 1.5 1.5), the result 3.0 must survive the park/resume as float. I'll write a gate-5 test: create a stream via stream/make + put!, read parks... Actually simpler reuse: a two-line session where line 1 defines a fn and line 2 calls it with float math — definitions crossing rounds IS state crossing but not continuation. The parked read is the real continuation boundary. Let me model on lines 256-280. Let me read that test.","signature":"b2e14c4e1fe4478196309f72"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a","uuid":"bb4b8533-f378-4600-83b3-42684ceb2d77","timestamp":"2026-11-26T12:55:44.225Z","thinking_duration_ms":4404,"thinking_display":"updates"}
